 \begin{enumerate}
-    \item \textbf{Request:} Vehicle $i$ at cell $c$ requests the next cell $c+1$ (or a lateral cell, if executing a lane change). The request is placed at time $\Treq(c+1, i) = \Tarr(c, i) + \ell / v^{(i)}$, where $v^{(i)}$ is the current speed.
+    \item \textbf{Request:} Vehicle $i$ at cell $c$ requests the next cell $c+1$ (or a lateral cell, if executing a lane change) as soon as it arrives in cell $c$: $\Treq(c+1, i) = \Tarr(c, i)$. Requesting on arrival rather than on reaching the cell boundary is what lets a vehicle in free flow cross cell after cell at $\ell / v^{(i)}$ seconds each: the next cell is already held when the vehicle gets there, and the queue in front of an occupied cell is served in request order.
 
     \item \textbf{Wait:} If cell $c+1$ is occupied (resource seized by another vehicle), vehicle $i$ waits. The DES scheduler suspends the vehicle's process until the resource becomes available.
 
