-\paragraph{Throughput.} Network throughput increases monotonically with AV penetration, from $4{,}494 \pm 14$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 0\%$ to $6{,}664 \pm 48$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 70\%$, a 48\% improvement. The confidence intervals are tight (coefficient of variation $< 1.6\%$ in all scenarios), confirming that 20 replications provide sufficient statistical resolution. The improvement is below the theoretical 71\% capacity increase (Table~\ref{tab:theoretical_capacity}), which assumes homogeneous mixed traffic without lane-change friction. The gap is attributable to ramp interactions and mandatory lane changes---vehicles destined for off-ramps must merge to the rightmost lane, creating cross-lane friction irrespective of vehicle type. Figure~\ref{fig:throughput_bar} visualizes the throughput and delay trends with 95\% confidence intervals.
+\paragraph{Throughput.} Network throughput increases with AV penetration, from $5{,}874 \pm 19$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 0\%$ to $6{,}983 \pm 34$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 70\%$, an 18.9\% improvement, and the confidence intervals are tight (coefficient of variation 0.7--1.1\%), so 20 replications resolve the differences comfortably. Most of the gain is already realised at 30\% AV ($6{,}679 \pm 25$, that is 13.7\%), and the last two scenarios are within 0.5\% of each other. The reason is that the network runs out of demand before it runs out of capacity: mainline and ramps offer 7{,}000\,veh/h, so $6{,}983$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 70\%$ is 99.8\% of everything that asks to enter. The all-HDV baseline, by contrast, serves 83.9\% of the offered demand and holds the rest back in the origin queues. The measured improvement is therefore a lower bound on the capacity effect: the theoretical 71\% capacity increase (Table~\ref{tab:theoretical_capacity}) cannot show up in a throughput figure that the demand caps. Where the extra capacity does show up is in delay, which keeps falling after throughput has saturated. Figure~\ref{fig:throughput_bar} visualizes the throughput and delay trends with 95\% confidence intervals.
 
-\paragraph{Delay.} Average delay drops from $254.1 \pm 6.6$\,s to $27.6 \pm 1.1$\,s as AV penetration increases from 0\% to 70\%, an 89\% reduction. The high baseline delay reflects the congested operating conditions: the per-lane OD flows with destination distributions require substantial lane-changing activity, and the combined mainline and ramp demand (7{,}000\,veh/h) loads the network near its effective capacity. The event-driven driver process enables vehicles to respond immediately to changing gaps rather than waiting for fixed evaluation intervals, and at 70\% AV penetration the shorter headway ($\tau = 0.5$\,s vs.\ 1.5\,s) significantly reduces car-following delays throughout the network. Figure~\ref{fig:travel_time} presents the average travel time across scenarios.
